WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.559
In this lesson we walk through the three decomposition patterns you'll see in almost

2
00:00:04.559 --> 00:00:11.119
every agentic system. Hierarchical, sequential, and parallel. We'll look at how each one works,

3
00:00:11.119 --> 00:00:17.120
when to apply it, how real systems combine all three, and which anti-patterns to watch out for.

4
00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:22.879
Let's get into it. Almost every decomposition you'll ever design is one of three shapes,

5
00:00:22.879 --> 00:00:29.520
or a mix of them. Hierarchical decomposition recursively splits goals into sub-goals

6
00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:35.439
across multiple levels, creating a tree where each level handles only its own tier of complexity.

7
00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:41.680
Sequential execution runs tasks in a defined order, each one potentially depending on the

8
00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:48.159
output of the step before it, and that dependency is exactly what forces the sequence. Parallel

9
00:00:48.159 --> 00:00:54.000
execution runs independent tasks at the same time, cutting total wall clock time,

10
00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:59.520
but introducing the need for synchronization when the branches converge back into the main workflow.

11
00:01:00.080 --> 00:01:05.680
Keep those shapes distinct in your head because real systems mix all of them, and you need to

12
00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:12.239
see which one applies where. Hierarchical decomposition builds a goal tree. At the top

13
00:01:12.239 --> 00:01:19.839
you have the high level goal. Below it, sub-goals. Below those, tasks. Below those, individual steps.

14
00:01:19.839 --> 00:01:25.199
The orchestrator at each level holds only its tier of complexity, and doesn't need to know the

15
00:01:25.199 --> 00:01:31.599
details of how lower levels execute. It delegates down and waits for results to flow back up.

16
00:01:31.599 --> 00:01:38.000
This mirrors how real organizations delegate work. An executive sets a strategic goal, a manager

17
00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:43.599
breaks it into projects, a team breaks those projects into tasks, and individuals handle

18
00:01:43.599 --> 00:01:49.599
specific steps. The advantage of this structure is specialization at each level. The risk is that

19
00:01:49.599 --> 00:01:55.839
coordination overhead grows with every layer you add, and errors at the bottom have to travel all

20
00:01:55.839 --> 00:02:01.279
the way back up before they're handled. One of the most important design decisions with hierarchical

21
00:02:01.279 --> 00:02:09.119
decomposition is how deep to go. Going deeper adds specialization. You can route sub-goals to agents

22
00:02:09.119 --> 00:02:14.240
that are purpose-built for that specific tier of complexity, and they don't have to deal with the

23
00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:20.160
broader context above them. But each added level increases what's called the error propagation

24
00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:26.000
surface. An error at the bottom of a five-level hierarchy has to travel back up through four

25
00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:31.360
orchestration layers before it's caught. Each of those layers is a chance for the error to be

26
00:02:31.360 --> 00:02:37.919
misinterpreted or swallowed. Flatten wherever you can. If two adjacent levels would run the same

27
00:02:37.919 --> 00:02:43.759
type of agent with nearly the same prompt, there's no reason for the separation. Depth should earn

28
00:02:43.759 --> 00:02:49.360
its cost through genuine specialization gains, not just because it looks clean on a diagram.

29
00:02:50.559 --> 00:02:56.559
Sequential decomposition is the straightforward pattern. Step 1 runs, produces output. Step 2

30
00:02:56.559 --> 00:03:03.199
takes that output as input, produces its own output, and so on down the chain. The key characteristic

31
00:03:03.199 --> 00:03:10.399
is that step order is enforced by data dependency. You can't run step 3 before step 2 because step 3

32
00:03:10.399 --> 00:03:16.720
needs what step 2 produces. Sequential patterns are the simplest to reason about and debug. When

33
00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:22.320
something goes wrong, you know exactly which step failed and what state the workflow was in. The

34
00:03:22.320 --> 00:03:28.160
trade-off is that sequential chains can't exploit any parallelism, and total time equals the sum of

35
00:03:28.160 --> 00:03:34.559
all step durations. When you're choosing between sequential and parallel, the deciding factor is

36
00:03:34.559 --> 00:03:41.600
always data dependency. If step B needs step A's output to function, it's sequential by necessity.

37
00:03:41.600 --> 00:03:47.679
If steps B and C both need step A's output but don't need anything from each other, they can

38
00:03:47.679 --> 00:03:54.720
run in parallel after A completes. Using sequential execution when steps are actually independent is a

39
00:03:54.720 --> 00:04:01.039
missed optimization. You're adding latency for no reliability benefit. But the reverse mistake

40
00:04:01.039 --> 00:04:08.399
is worse. Treating dependent steps as if they're parallel causes data hazards. Step B starts before

41
00:04:08.399 --> 00:04:15.759
it has the input it needs, uses stale or missing data, and produces wrong output. Dependency analysis

42
00:04:15.759 --> 00:04:22.079
has to come first. Before you decide on execution pattern, map out what each step actually needs.

43
00:04:23.359 --> 00:04:28.480
Parallel decomposition identifies subtasks that have no data dependencies on each other

44
00:04:28.480 --> 00:04:34.160
and runs them concurrently. This is where you recover the latency that sequential patterns

45
00:04:34.160 --> 00:04:39.359
sacrifice. If you have five independent research tasks that each take 10 seconds,

46
00:04:39.920 --> 00:04:47.440
sequential execution takes 50 seconds. Parallel takes 10. The design requirements are strict.

47
00:04:47.440 --> 00:04:53.679
Each parallel branch has to be truly independent with no shared mutable state. If two branches

48
00:04:53.679 --> 00:04:59.279
write to the same resource without coordination, you get race conditions. When all branches

49
00:04:59.279 --> 00:05:06.239
complete, a fan-in step collects results and continues the workflow. Parallel execution

50
00:05:06.239 --> 00:05:13.200
introduces a failure scenario. Sequential patterns avoid partial failure where some branches succeed

51
00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:19.359
and others don't. Your orchestration logic has to handle that explicitly with a defined policy for

52
00:05:19.359 --> 00:05:26.640
what happens when the fan-in collects incomplete results. Real systems combine all three patterns

53
00:05:26.640 --> 00:05:32.000
and seeing them together is more representative of actual architectures than studying each one

54
00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:38.079
in isolation. A hierarchical orchestrator might delegate to a pair of specialized sub-agents

55
00:05:38.079 --> 00:05:43.519
running in parallel where each sub-agent internally runs a sequential pipeline of steps

56
00:05:44.160 --> 00:05:51.440
or a sequential pipeline might fan out to parallel branches for a batch operation, then fan back in

57
00:05:51.440 --> 00:05:57.519
before the next stage. The decision at each point is local. What does this specific segment of the

58
00:05:57.519 --> 00:06:04.640
workflow need? Sequential, where dependencies exist, parallel where they don't, and hierarchical,

59
00:06:04.640 --> 00:06:10.959
where specialization justifies the layering. The most reliable architectures pick the right pattern

60
00:06:11.040 --> 00:06:16.160
for each segment based on its structure, not based on a preference for any one approach.

61
00:06:17.440 --> 00:06:23.679
Three anti-patterns show up repeatedly in agentic systems and all three are worth recognizing

62
00:06:23.679 --> 00:06:29.600
on site. Over-decomposition happens when you've added so many steps and handoffs that the cost of

63
00:06:29.600 --> 00:06:35.839
coordinating them exceeds the cost of just running the task as a single unit. The workflow spends

64
00:06:35.839 --> 00:06:42.160
more time passing context around than doing work. Under-decomposition is the opposite. Tasks are so

65
00:06:42.160 --> 00:06:48.320
large that a single failure kills the whole workflow and retry means starting from scratch,

66
00:06:48.320 --> 00:06:54.079
and false parallelism is the most dangerous. Treating steps with data dependencies as if

67
00:06:54.079 --> 00:06:59.839
they're independent, running them in parallel, and getting data hazards and silent incorrect outputs.

68
00:07:00.399 --> 00:07:06.559
When you see a failing system, diagnosing which anti-pattern it has tells you exactly what kind

69
00:07:06.559 --> 00:07:13.679
of fix is needed. A pattern isn't a preference you bring into a workflow. It's a reading of what the

70
00:07:13.679 --> 00:07:19.760
dependencies already are. Parallel describes steps that don't need each other. It isn't a wish for

71
00:07:19.760 --> 00:07:26.160
things to run faster. Confuse those two and you get false parallelism. It fails quietly and hands

72
00:07:26.160 --> 00:07:32.079
you the wrong answers instead of giving you what you're going for. That wraps up this one. I'll see

73
00:07:32.079 --> 00:07:33.119
you in the next lesson.

